      <div class="foot mono cover-foot">
        <span>{ed.dateLabel} · {SITE.author}</span>
        <span>r = cos(kθ) · seed {ed.seed} · CoreWeave, 30 Jun 2026</span>
      </div>
    </section>
    <FilmPlayer slug={ed.slug} />

    <section class="surface flash" id="thesis" aria-label="Thesis">
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="c-full mono-b">Thesis</p>
        <p class="c-full display thin slap">The length is ordinary. The weight is not.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Section n="00" title="Seventy-two on five" id="number">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="num figure fig-m">$72.0B</p>
        <p class="mono fig-sub">Liabilities at 30 June 2026, against $5.0 billion of equity <span class="cite">{c('q2')}</span></p>
        <p>CoreWeave rents out Nvidia’s processors, by the hour and by the year, to the companies that train and run AI models. At 30 June 2026, the last date it has filed, it held $77.1 billion of assets against $72.0 billion of liabilities. What belonged to its shareholders was $5.0 billion. <span class="cite">{c('q2')}</span></p>
        <p>Of the liabilities, $35.1 billion is debt and $16.5 billion is leases on data centres already in use. Those two together are the $52 billion that has been printed as its debt and lease hole. The rest include $9.7 billion its customers have paid in advance. <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'fortune')}</span></p>
        <p>A further $35.5 billion is rent on leases it has signed and not yet begun. That is not a liability. It is an estimate of future payments, undiscounted, and it does not add to the other two: they are present values and it is not. <span class="cite">{c('q2')}</span></p>
        <p>Since June it has drawn $1.2 billion of a new $2.6 billion loan facility and $1.2 billion of its revolving credit line, sold $4.2 billion of convertible notes and opened a programme to sell up to 35 million new shares, while $4.4 billion of its debt falls due before the year is out. It has filed nothing for the third quarter, so no current total exists. <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'ddtl55', 'conv', 'atm')}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b">
        <dl class="rail">
          <dt>Filed</dt><dd>An SEC filing, a statute, a court record, an official statistic or a published paper. A paragraph under no mark is filed.</dd>
          <dt>Reported</dt><dd>A named outlet or association, read where it opened. Where only a relay opened, the entry names the relay.</dd>
          <dt>Estimate</dt><dd>Someone’s own model, named.</dd>
        </dl>
        <Stats class="stack" items={[
          ['$35.1B', `debt, carrying value ${c('q2')}`],
          ['$16.5B', `lease liabilities, data centres in use ${c('q2')}`],
          ['$35.5B', `rent on leases signed, not begun; not a liability ${c('q2')}`],
          ['$5.0B', `equity ${c('q2')}`],
        ]} />
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="01" title="Atlantic Crypto Corp" ground="bottle" acid id="origin">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>It was founded in September 2017. Its first name on the SEC’s record is Atlantic Crypto Corp, and it became CoreWeave in October 2019. It mined cryptocurrency until 30 September 2022, when Ethereum changed its rules, and before 2022 most of its small revenue came, in its own words, from “our past crypto mining offerings, which were discontinued”. <span class="cite">{c('edgar', 'ipo', 'k25')}</span></p>
        <p>Its founders came from energy trading. Michael Intrator co-founded and ran Hudson Ridge Asset Management, a natural gas hedge fund, where Brian Venturo was a partner. Brannin McBee was a proprietary trader at Windy Bay Power, a commodity-focused hedge fund. <span class="cite">{c('proxy', 'ipo')}</span></p>
        <p>It is now in the Nasdaq-100. It reported $5.1 billion of revenue for 2025 and $4.65 billion for the first half of 2026, and at 30 June held $103.7 billion of contracted work not yet delivered. Ninety-eight percent of its revenue comes from committed contracts, which are take-or-pay: the customer pays for the capacity whether it uses it or not. <span class="cite">{c('k25', 'q2', 'rel')}</span></p>
        <p>In its own words, “We currently lease all of our existing data centers and offices.” Its one interest in real property is a 35 percent stake in a joint venture developing a data centre campus at Kenilworth, New Jersey, with up to $1.7 billion committed to two more. What it owns is what goes inside: $46.7 billion of servers, networking and other equipment, net of depreciation, 61 percent of its assets, much of it pledged to its lenders. <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'k25')}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b prose side">
        <p class="mono-b">Not a contractor</p>
        <p>It owns the machines. It rents almost everything around them.</p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="02" title="Every four dollars" acid id="dollars">
      <RoseField layers={div(4, 9, 0.22)} class="rose divider" />
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>In the second quarter of 2026 CoreWeave billed $2.58 billion. Its operating costs came to $2.62 billion, a loss of $49 million before interest. Net interest was $640 million, and the quarter’s net loss was $626 million. <span class="cite">{c('q2')}</span></p>
        <p>Scaled to every $4.00 it billed: about $2.02 went to depreciation on servers and networking, $1.37 to the cost of revenue, which is data centre rent, power and the facilities’ own equipment, $0.99 to interest and $0.69 to everything else. That is $5.07 of cost and interest for $4.00 of revenue. After other income and tax it lost $0.97. <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'rel')}</span></p>
        <p>The split is rebuilt from the filing’s own lines. Its depreciation figure is the filing’s “approximately $1.3 billion” for servers and networking in the quarter; the rest of the quarter’s $1.39 billion of depreciation sits inside the cost of revenue. <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'rel')}</span></p>
        <p>It publishes no gross margin. Computed, revenue less the cost of revenue was 66 percent in the quarter, and it reads high because the depreciation of servers and networking sits below that line. The margins it does publish are minus 2 percent operating and minus 24 percent net; on its own adjusted measures, 59 percent EBITDA and 5 percent operating. <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'rel')}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b">
        <figure class="field bars" aria-label="Of every 4 dollars billed in the second quarter of 2026, what it went to">
          <figcaption class="mono">Of every $4.00 billed · second quarter 2026 <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'rel')}</span></figcaption>
          <div class="bar"><span class="mono lbl">Billed</span><span class="track"><span class="fill" style="width:100%"></span></span><span class="num val">$4.00</span></div>
          {dollars.map(([l, v]) => (
            <div class="bar"><span class="mono lbl">{l}</span><span class="track"><span class="fill" style={`width:${(v / 4) * 100}%`}></span></span><span class="num val">${v.toFixed(2)}</span></div>
          ))}
          <p class="mono note">Spent $5.07. Lost $0.97, after other income and tax.</p>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="03" title="Five years against <span class='acid'>eleven</span>" ground="bottle" id="tail">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>The contracts are shorter than the leases. At the end of 2025 CoreWeave’s committed contracts had, in its words, “a weighted-average contract duration of approximately five years”, and its leases in use had eleven years left to run on average. <span class="cite">{c('k25')}</span></p>
        <p>By 30 June 2026 the leases in use had twelve years left. Of the $103.7 billion of work under contract that day, 41 percent was due within two years, 80 percent within four, and all of it by month 78. Leases signed and not yet begun run seven to sixteen years. <span class="cite">{c('q2')}</span></p>
        <p>The contracts are getting shorter. The $2.6 billion facility of August is, CoreWeave says, the first of its equipment loans not matched to the contracts behind it: about five years of debt against contracts that “average approximately three years in length”. In September it told investors that since June it had signed customer contracts “with terms of approximately three to six months”. <span class="cite">{c('ddtl55', 'atm')}</span></p>
        <p>Customers pay part in advance: across its active contracts the weighted-average prepayment was 15 to 25 percent of total contract value at the end of 2025. <span class="cite">{c('k25')}</span></p>
        <p>The length is not unusual. Equinix, which owns some of its data centres and leases the rest, reports the same twelve-year average remaining lease term, against customer contracts “generally one to five years in length”. Microsoft disclosed $329.1 billion of leases, mostly data centres, signed and not yet begun, running one to twenty years. <span class="cite">{c('eqix', 'msft')}</span></p>
        <p>The weight is. Set against each company’s own balance sheet, CoreWeave’s lease liabilities are more than three times its equity, where Equinix’s are about a quarter of its and Microsoft’s a fifth, and the leases it has signed and not begun come to seven times its equity. Equinix has more than 10,500 customers, and its largest brings in about 3 percent of its recurring revenue. <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'eqix', 'msft')}</span></p>
        <p>And the equipment wears out first. CoreWeave depreciates its servers over six years, so the machines a twelve-year lease was signed to hold are written off twice over before it ends. <span class="cite">{c('k25')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Reported</p>
        <p>Jeffrey Moerdler, a data centre lawyer at Haynes Boone, told a Bisnow summit in July that neoclouds are “signing 15-year data center leases” against customer agreements of “max one to three years”. CoreWeave’s own bear him out: those it signed itself with Applied Digital in 2025, about $7 billion, run about fifteen years. <span class="cite">{c('bis1', 'apld')}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b">
        <figure class="field bars" aria-label="Contract, equipment and lease lengths in years, each at its own date">
          <figcaption class="mono">Years, each at its own date <span class="cite">{c('k25', 'q2')}</span></figcaption>
          {years.map(([l, v]) => (
            <div class="bar"><span class="mono lbl">{l}</span><span class="track"><span class="fill" style={`width:${(v / 16) * 100}%`}></span></span><span class="num val">{v}</span></div>
          ))}
        </figure>
        <div class="field scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Leases against each company’s own balance sheet">
          <table class="ledger">
            <caption class="mono">Leases against each company’s own balance sheet · computed from each filing <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'eqix', 'msft')}</span></caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">CoreWeave · Jun 2026</th><th scope="col">Equinix · Dec 2025</th><th scope="col">Microsoft · Jun 2026</th></tr></thead>
