              {ratios.map(([l, a, b, d]) => (<tr><th scope="row">{l}</th><td class="n">{a}</td><td class="n">{b}</td><td class="n">{d}</td></tr>))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="04" title="Three names" id="names">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="num figure fig-m">72%</p>
        <p class="mono fig-sub">of revenue from three customers, second quarter 2026 <span class="cite">{c('q2')}</span></p>
        <p>In 2025 one customer, Microsoft, was 67 percent of CoreWeave’s revenue. In the second quarter of 2026 its three largest customers were 36, 26 and 10 percent of it. <span class="cite">{c('k25', 'q2')}</span></p>
        <p>Two of the larger contracts are public in outline. OpenAI committed in March 2025 to pay up to about $11.9 billion through October 2030, and in September 2025 up to about $6.5 billion more through May 2031. Meta committed in March 2026 to pay up to about $21.0 billion. <span class="cite">{c('k25', 'q2')}</span></p>
        <p>CoreWeave’s bond documents say how its lenders weigh them. The indenture for its 2032 notes lets a financing vehicle borrow up to 90 percent of its value where its contracts are mostly with investment-grade customers and 75 percent where they are not, and it names one contract: OpenAI’s, at 75 percent, or 90 to the extent OpenAI has an investment-grade rating, with debt against that contract capped at $7.0 billion. Its largest customer, Microsoft, is rated at the top of the scale. <span class="cite">{c('ind')}</span></p>
        <p class="beat">The unusual risk is not how long the leases run. It is how much rests on so little equity, and on so few customers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b">
        <Stats class="stack" items={[
          ['$11.9B', `OpenAI, up to, through Oct 2030 ${c('k25')}`],
          ['$6.5B', `OpenAI, up to, through May 2031 ${c('q2')}`],
          ['$21.0B', `Meta, up to, from Mar 2026 ${c('q2')}`],
        ]} />
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="05" title="The price of a name" ground="bottle" acid id="price">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>What the lenders price is the customer. CoreWeave’s filed loan agreements lend a fixed share of the cost of the equipment, and the customer’s credit rating sets the share. Its $8.5 billion loan of March 2026, secured by its equipment and one investment-grade customer’s contract, lends 90 percent; Moody’s rated it A3, the first loan of its kind rated investment grade, and it priced at 2.25 percentage points over SOFR, though the parent guarantees it only against fraud, misconduct and a voluntary bankruptcy. Its $3.1 billion loan of May, backed by contracts with “two large, non-investment grade customers”, lends 71.42 percent, carries the parent’s full guarantee, was rated Ba2, and still priced at 4.50 points over. In 2025 its loans against OpenAI’s contracts lent 80 percent, then 75. <span class="cite">{c('ddtl4', 'ddtl5', 'ddtl5ca', 'ddtl3', 'ddtl2', 'q2')}</span></p>
        <p>The August facility, over contracts of about three years and guaranteed by the parent, was rated Ba2 by Moody’s and BB+ by Fitch and priced at 5.50 points over. <span class="cite">{c('ddtl55')}</span></p>
        <p>Blackstone’s credit arm anchored the March loan, which also requires a projected debt service coverage of at least 1.20 to 1. <span class="cite">{c('ddtl4')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Estimate</p>
        <p>The 70 to 80 percent in circulation is SemiAnalysis’s estimate, from its cost model. The filed figures run from 60 to 90. <span class="cite">{c('semi', 'ddtl2', 'ddtl4')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Reported</p>
        <p>The parent’s own debt is priced on its own name. On 15 October 2026 CoreWeave’s five-year credit default swaps touched a record 1,194.5 basis points, a price implying better than even odds of default within five years, by ICE Data Services’ measure as reported by Bloomberg. The same day Jensen Huang, reported as saying CoreWeave needed no backstop from Nvidia, said: “Their cost of capital is actually decreasing.” <span class="cite">{c('huang')}</span></p>
        <p>JPMorgan’s analysts wrote, according to Bloomberg as reported by Bisnow, that “the cash burn is not a function of unfavorable economics per project but more of the capacity being ramped each year relative to the installed active capacity at this time”. <span class="cite">{c('bis2')}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b">
        <div class="field scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Three loans against equipment and contracts">
          <table class="ledger">
            <caption class="mono">Three loans against equipment and contracts, 2026 <span class="cite">{c('ddtl4', 'ddtl5', 'ddtl5ca', 'ddtl55', 'q2')}</span></caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Loan</th><th scope="col">Closed</th><th scope="col">Rated</th><th scope="col">Over SOFR</th><th scope="col">Lends, of cost</th><th scope="col">Parent guarantees it</th><th scope="col">Behind it</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {loans.map(([a, d, r, s, l, g, b]) => (<tr><th scope="row">{a}</th><td>{d}</td><td>{r}</td><td class="n">{s}</td><td class="n">{l}</td><td>{g}</td><td>{b}</td></tr>))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <section class="surface flash" id="words" aria-label="In its own words">
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="c-full display slap">“the strong credit quality of many of our customers”</p>
        <p class="c-full mono">CoreWeave, on what its financing lets it borrow against · 10-K for 2025 · {c('k25')}</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Section n="06" title="Who holds the lien" ground="bottle" acid id="lien">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>Much of the equipment sits in what CoreWeave calls “bankruptcy-remote, special-purpose consolidated subsidiaries formed to hold the financed infrastructure and the related customer contracts”. At 30 June they held $18.2 billion of long-lived assets and $2.6 billion of current assets, pledged to lenders. <span class="cite">{c('q2')}</span></p>
        <p>Most of the liens belong to the lenders. The March facility is secured by “perfected first priority pledges” over the subsidiary’s equity and substantially all of its assets, held for the lenders by a collateral agent, and the customer signs a consent with that agent whose terms are not filed. <span class="cite">{c('ddtl4', 'q2')}</span></p>
        <p>One customer held one. CoreWeave’s quarterly reports for the first three quarters of 2025 said of the vehicle built to serve OpenAI: “Upon an event of default, OpenAI has a lien and security interest in the equity of the special purpose vehicle.” Its annual report for 2025 and the reports since no longer repeat the sentence, which is not evidence that the lien ended. No filing or report found gives Meta one. <span class="cite">{c('q225q', 'k25', 'q2', 'meta')}</span></p>
        <p>The loan that financed that vehicle is, by its own terms, subject “in all respects” to an unfiled agreement among OpenAI, Dell Financial Services and the lenders’ agent, which sets the order of payment after a trigger it defines. The servers in it are not the lenders’ collateral; they are leased from Dell Financial Services. And CoreWeave’s guarantee covers only part of that loan, and falls away if OpenAI becomes investment grade. <span class="cite">{c('ddtl3')}</span></p>
        <p>The wall around the subsidiaries is thin. Of $35.1 billion of debt, $31.4 billion is recourse to the parent, its own or guaranteed by it, and $3.7 billion, chiefly the March loan, is not. Behind the pledged assets stand about $14.6 billion of the parent’s unsecured notes. <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'ddtl55')}</span></p>
        <p>Meta stands inside vehicles like these too, its own landlords’, and stands there as guarantor. It owns 20 percent of the two ventures developing its Louisiana and Texas campuses, leases them, and guarantees their investors up to $41.5 billion of the properties’ residual value, against which it has recorded no liability. It has also agreed to buy up to $14.72 billion of an unnamed cloud provider’s capacity over five years, reducible if the provider sells it to others: a floor of the kind Nvidia gives CoreWeave. <span class="cite">{c('meta')}</span></p>
        <p>If a tenant fails, the law caps what a long lease is worth to its landlord. A bankrupt tenant has 120 days, extendable once by 90, to keep or reject a lease of commercial property, paying the rent in full meanwhile, and the landlord’s claim for the rest is capped at the greater of a year’s rent or 15 percent of what remains, never more than three years’. Most of a long lease rejected in a default would fall on landlords and their lenders. <span class="cite">{c('usc')}</span></p>
        <p>Not all of it. CoreWeave, Inc. signs some leases itself, the Applied Digital ones among them, and about 90 percent of its debt is its own or guaranteed by it. The capped rent, and any shortfall on that debt, would rank beside its bondholders. <span class="cite">{c('apld', 'q2')}</span></p>
        <p>Its finance chief’s case is that the debt is paid off inside the contracts: “So when an initial contract ends, the cluster no longer has any leverage”. The August facility is the first that was not, by its own terms. <span class="cite">{c('call', 'ddtl55')}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b">
        <dl class="rail">
          <dt>The lenders</dt><dd>Most of the liens, through a collateral agent.</dd>
          <dt>OpenAI</dt><dd>Shares in CoreWeave, and by its 2025 reports a lien on one vehicle’s equity on default.</dd>
          <dt>Dell Financial Services</dt><dd>The servers in that vehicle, leased.</dd>
          <dt>Meta, elsewhere</dt><dd>A guarantee to its landlords’ investors.</dd>
          <dt>The noteholders</dt><dd>What remains.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="07" title="The supplier as buyer" acid id="nvidia">
      <RoseField layers={div(6, 11, 0.67)} class="rose divider low" />
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>Every processor CoreWeave runs today is Nvidia’s. It buys them in systems from manufacturers and integrators: three suppliers made up 23, 20 and 17 percent of its purchases in 2025. In January 2026 Nvidia bought about 23 million new CoreWeave shares at $87.20, $2.0 billion in all. <span class="cite">{c('k25', 'q2')}</span></p>
        <p>In September 2025 the two signed an order form with “an initial value of $6.3 billion” under which Nvidia must buy CoreWeave’s “residual unsold” capacity through 13 April 2032, “subject to any termination described below and satisfaction of delivery and availability of service requirements”. Either side may end it on 30 days’ notice of a breach. It is a floor under unsold capacity, not a promise of $6.3 billion. <span class="cite">{c('nvof')}</span></p>
        <p>Nvidia’s own filing is larger and does not mention CoreWeave. At 26 July 2026 it carried $56 billion of commitments, $36 billion of them cloud agreements of the same residual kind, and guarantees with a maximum exposure of $108.5 billion. Of that, $105 billion backs lease and power payments on a 4.25 gigawatt campus in Ohio whose tenant is an affiliate of OpenAI, and OpenAI has agreed to reimburse Nvidia for anything it pays. <span class="cite">{c('nvq', 'nv8k')}</span></p>
        <p>Add the two tables and you have the $164.5 billion that Forbes printed in October. The filing never adds them, because one is a schedule of purchases and the other a ceiling on guarantees. <span class="cite">{c('forbes', 'nvq')}</span></p>
        <p>In August Nvidia said it would work with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to raise more than $500 billion for AI infrastructure. The release says memorandums of understanding were signed and remain “subject to execution of the final agreements”. It promises no guarantee. <span class="cite">{c('apollo')}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b">
        <figure class="field bars" aria-label="Nvidia's commitments and maximum guarantee exposure, quarter ended 26 July 2026, billions of dollars">
          <figcaption class="mono">Nvidia, 26 Jul 2026 · $ billions · two tables, never summed <span class="cite">{c('nvq')}</span></figcaption>
          {nvidia.map(([l, v]) => (
            <div class="bar"><span class="mono lbl">{l}</span><span class="track"><span class="fill" style={`width:${(v / 105) * 100}%`}></span></span><span class="num val">{v}</span></div>
          ))}
        </figure>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="08" title="A loss is not a cash flow" ground="bottle" acid id="labs">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>At the top of the chain are the companies that buy the compute. In 2025 all three reported large losses and spent more cash than they took in. The two are not the same number, and most of the difference is one accounting line. <span class="cite">{c('s1', 'zit', 'spx')}</span></p>
        <p>Anthropic’s net loss for 2025 was $42.0 billion. Of it, $34.3 billion was the rise in the fair value of convertible notes, most of them Amazon’s, which, in Anthropic’s words, “was primarily driven by an increase in the fair value of our outstanding convertible promissory notes resulting from an increase in our enterprise valuation from 2024 to 2025.” It costs no cash. It is paid in shares: in the first quarter of 2026 Amazon’s notes converted at $20.93 a share, while Series G-1 investors had paid $259.14. <span class="cite">{c('s1')}</span></p>
        <p>So a charge like that is not a cash flow, and it is not nothing either. It measures value moving from the other shareholders to the noteholders. What it does not measure is money spent. <span class="cite">{c('s1')}</span></p>
        <p>Measured as money spent, Anthropic’s operating loss for 2025 was $8.06 billion, a $1.5 billion legal settlement among it, and its operating cash flow less capital spending was minus $2.77 billion. In its own words it has “generated negative cash flows from operating and investing activities in 2023, 2024, and 2025.” In the first half of 2026 its operating loss narrowed to $693 million and its operations brought in $3.93 billion. SpaceX, which owns xAI and has been listed since June, took in $6.79 billion from its operations in 2025 and spent $20.74 billion on equipment. <span class="cite">{c('s1', 'spx')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Reported</p>
        <p>OpenAI has filed nothing public. Its 2025 accounts reached Ed Zitron and the Financial Times, and Fortune reported from the same copies: one origin. They show $20.92 billion of operating loss on $13.07 billion of revenue and a consolidated net loss of $60.35 billion; in Zitron’s words, the year’s conversion to a for-profit company led to “a $41.55 billion loss due to changes in fair value of convertible interests and warrant liability.” After $21.82 billion assigned to other holders, $38.53 billion was OpenAI’s own. Its own plan, reported by the Financial Times, has it burning $278 billion of free cash flow from 2026 to 2030. <span class="cite">{c('zit', 'fort2', 'ft278')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Filed</p>
        <p>Neither private one has a market price. Anthropic filed to list on 26 October with its price range left blank, and last priced in a $65 billion round it announced on 28 May, at $965 billion including the new money. SpaceX listed on 12 June at $135 a share, which on its share count valued it at $1.77 trillion. <span class="cite">{c('s1', 'seriesh', 'spx')}</span></p>
        <p>Anthropic’s filing leaves its total committed spend blank. It discloses $265.6 billion of fixed or minimum purchase commitments and $321.4 billion of rent on leases not yet begun, both at 30 June 2026, two measures that do not add. Its arrangements with SpaceX’s AI unit, up to $120.5 billion, can be cancelled by either side on 90 days’ notice once their first periods end in January and March 2027. <span class="cite">{c('s1')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Reported</p>
        <p>The two trillion dollars in the press is a figure Reuters reported on 29 September that Anthropic “could” reach; no draft of the filing contains it. The same Reuters sentence gave the $518 billion of commitments in circulation, which matches six arrangements named in the drafts from August to October; the same six in the filed document come to $571.1 billion, and two of them are not firm. OpenAI last priced at $852 billion, including the new money, in March 2026, and CNBC reported on 22 October that it was raising at $1.25 trillion before the new money, terms still under negotiation. <span class="cite">{c('reut', 'drs', 's1', 'qz', 'tc')}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-full field scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Three companies’ 2025 accounts">
        <table class="ledger">
          <caption class="mono">2025 · $ billions · OpenAI’s figures leaked, not filed, its net loss consolidated · the last column computed from each filing’s cash flow statement <span class="cite">{c('zit', 'fort2', 's1', 'spx')}</span></caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Company</th><th scope="col">Revenue</th><th scope="col">Operating loss</th><th scope="col">Net loss</th><th scope="col">Of which, fair-value charge</th><th scope="col">Operating cash less capital spending</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {labs.map(([n, r, o, l, f, cf]) => (<tr><th scope="row">{n}</th><td class="n">{r}</td><td class="n">{o}</td><td class="n">{l}</td><td class="n">{f}</td><td class="n">{cf}</td></tr>))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="09" title="Contracted, not energised" acid id="power">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>At 30 June 2026 CoreWeave had about 3.7 gigawatts of power under contract and 1.5 gigawatts of it active. The other 2.2, about 59 percent, was not yet switched on. <span class="cite">{c('rel')}</span></p>
        <p>The share has fallen from 79 percent a year earlier. The gap has not: it has sat between 2.2 and 2.5 gigawatts for a year, because CoreWeave has been signing about as fast as it switches on. It switched on nearly 500 megawatts in the second quarter of 2026, against about 50 a year before, and six weeks later its contracted total was 4.2 gigawatts. <span class="cite">{c('q225', 'q325', 'rel', 'call')}</span></p>
        <p>It does not say the rest waits on the grid. It calls it capacity “we expect to deploy over future periods”, its filings list limits on generation and transmission among the risks to its expansion without tying any capacity to them, and the one delay it has named was a third-party developer’s, announced in November 2025. Its annual report also says that “limited access to power will favor platforms like ours”. <span class="cite">{c('k25', 'q2')}</span></p>
        <p>The wait for power is real elsewhere. Dominion Energy told Virginia utilities in August 2024 that connecting a data centre drawing more than 100 megawatts would take one to three years longer than the three to four it had taken. JLL puts the average wait for a grid connection in primary markets above four years; by market it runs from about two years to about ten, Northern Virginia near seven, against 18 months to put up a 50 megawatt data centre. A substation transformer took about 140 weeks to arrive in 2023 and more than 160 in 2026, by Wood Mackenzie’s count. <span class="cite">{c('dom', 'jll', 'dck1')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Reported</p>
        <p>Satya Nadella, Microsoft’s chief executive, a year earlier: “you may actually have a bunch of chips sitting in inventory that I can’t plug in. And in fact, that is my problem today, right? It’s not a supply issue of chips. It’s actually the fact that I don’t have warm shells to plug into.” <span class="cite">{c('bg2')}</span></p>
        <p>In October 2026 Microsoft withdrew from its contract to take capacity at Nscale’s planned site at Loughton, in Essex, where 23,040 Nvidia processors were to run from early 2027. The Guardian reported that UK Power Networks had told Nscale it could not supply enough power until the 2030s; UK Power Networks told AFP that many data centres are “awaiting works on the national transmission network, which we do not own”. Microsoft said “this decision relates to a single site”, and Nscale still means to go ahead. <span class="cite">{c('nscale', 'dck2', 'afp')}</span></p>
        <p>Business Insider reported in October that, under what Amazon calls Early Rack Receipt, internal emails from August expected about $5 billion of Nvidia racks to arrive before they could be deployed, and about $3 billion more each month after. Some sites had room for the racks but not the power to run them, people familiar with it said, so finished equipment waited in other warehouses. Amazon called staging hardware near its sites best business practice. How much of it waits, nobody has said. <span class="cite">{c('bij', 'aws')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Filed</p>
        <p>Equipment not yet placed in service does not lose book value while it waits. It sits as construction in progress, $11.9 billion of it at CoreWeave on 30 June, undepreciated, with the interest on it capitalised; its finance chief tied the rise to “the large amount of power we received very late in Q2”. Equipment already in service and then left idle does keep depreciating. What waiting costs is ground against the next generation. <span class="cite">{c('q2', 'call')}</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b">
        <figure class="field bars" aria-label="CoreWeave's contracted power not yet active, gigawatts, by date">
          <figcaption class="mono">Contracted power not yet active · gigawatts <span class="cite">{c('q225', 'q325', 'k25', 'rel')}</span></figcaption>
          {power.map(([l, v, s]) => (
            <div class="bar"><span class="mono lbl">{l}</span><span class="track"><span class="fill" style={`width:${(v / 2.5) * 100}%`}></span></span><span class="num val">{s}</span></div>
          ))}
          <p class="mono note">As a share of contracted power: 79, 80, under 73 and 59 percent. 2.2 GW was also the whole contracted total in June 2025.</p>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="10" title="Credit, land and hands" ground="bottle" acid id="houses">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>The case that this boom crowds out housing starts with banks, and with one economist’s account of them. Richard Werner showed in 2014 that a bank creates new money when it lends, and the Bank of England said the same that year. His later work splits bank credit into credit for things that count in output and credit for buying existing assets, and blames the second for bubbles. On his own split, a loan to put up a data centre or buy servers is the first kind. <span class="cite">{c('w14', 'boe', 'w12')}</span></p>
        <p>Where banks do appear is in the loans themselves and behind the lenders that are not banks. The 2025 loan against OpenAI’s contracts was made directly by banks, MUFG, Morgan Stanley, Goldman Sachs and JPMorgan among them. Bank loans to lenders that are not banks, private credit and equity funds and securitisation vehicles among them, rose from $1.72 trillion to $2.06 trillion between September 2025 and September 2026, about a fifth, part of it reclassified into the line, while residential real estate lending grew under 2 percent. The figures do not say how much went to data centres. <span class="cite">{c('ddtl3', 'h8')}</span></p>
<p>The capital rule tilts toward housing, where it tilts at all. A mortgage on an occupied or rented home and a builder’s loan on a home already sold carry a 50 percent risk weight; other home construction loans carry 100. A construction loan on commercial property built for income, secured by it and repaid from it, carries 150, or 100 once the borrower has put in 15 percent of its finished value and the loan meets the rule’s other tests. Loans to the cloud companies themselves, or secured by servers and contracts rather than land, are corporate loans at 100. The one place homes and data centres meet is a supervisory screen of 2006 that counts all construction lending together and, in its own words, does “not constitute limits”. <span class="cite">{c('cfr', 'prop', 'cre')}</span></p>
        <p>Banks lent about $14.9 billion to data centres in the year to the third quarter of 2025, on MSCI’s estimate as cited by the Chicago Fed, which found the exposure not material from a commercial property view; most of large banks’ lending to AI is to companies, $450 billion committed and $150 billion drawn. Lending to build single-family homes was rising, 1.9 percent on the year in the first quarter of 2026. Builders have reported tighter credit for eighteen quarters running, a run that began before data centre spending took off in 2023. <span class="cite">{c('chi', 'nahb', 'feds')}</span></p>
        <p>Data centre construction ran at $85 billion a year in August 2026, against $519 billion for new homes, single-family and multifamily. The $370 billion a year in circulation is a Federal Reserve staff forecast of all data centre investment by mid 2026, the servers included, and its authors assume only 44 percent of it is domestic. <span class="cite">{c('c30', 'feds')}</span></p>
        <p>Large banks’ direct lending to AI-related industries was about 0.8 percent of their assets, by the Chicago Fed’s estimate, which found the indirect channels, through lenders that are not banks, hard to measure with the data regulators hold. The Kansas City Fed put neocloud companies at 85 percent debt to assets, against 44 for the largest cloud companies and 34 for chipmakers, and noted that the newest financing structures “have not yet been stress-tested by an economic downturn”. <span class="cite">{c('chi', 'kc')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Reported</p>
        <p>The Urban Institute, which raised the question in October, wrote that “to date, no study has quantified whether data center financing has diverted capital from housing.” For land it gave one case, a site “entitled for 516 homes” sold to Amazon, which it said sought the rezoning. It took the case from HousingWire. <span class="cite">{c('urban', 'hw')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Filed</p>
        <p>The court record has it the other way round. The applicant for the data centre rezoning was Stanley Martin, the homebuilder that had won the 516 homes; the county board granted it in November 2023, and the parties to the appeal were the board and Stanley Martin. Amazon bought most of the land afterwards. <span class="cite">{c('court', 'pwt', 'co')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Reported</p>
        <p>The squeeze on trades is real and local. The builders’ association put construction’s need at 349,000 more workers in 2026, down from 439,000 in 2025, for every trade together, most of it to replace people retiring, and says demand for electricians has surged with data centre construction. Near Abilene, Texas, one electrical contractor told the Texas Tribune he could pay $20 an hour while data centres paid $35, with overtime and per diem on top, and a builder said his houses were taking two months longer since his electrician lost lead men to them. <span class="cite">{c('abc', 'ttrib')}</span></p>
        <p class="mono reg">Filed</p>
        <p>An electrician’s median pay was $63,190 in May 2025, and the top tenth earned more than $108,510. <span class="cite">{c('bls')}</span></p>
      </div>
